WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.080 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in a disappointing contrast to its  stellar pixel drunk series cue games  latest downloadable PlayStation  exclusive the tomorrow children is a  bland monotonous inscrutable mess that  locks you into a tedious time draining  loop the game consists primarily of  resource gathering and city building  with some floaty platforming and clumsy  combat in-between but none of these  constituent parts feel satisfying on  their own and together they never amount  to a larger payoff to make matters worse  the game's opening tutorial doesn't  really explain what you're supposed to  do in tomorrow children let alone how or  why instead it covers only the absolute  basics following a failed global  experiment all the remains of our world  is the void a vast empty space dotted  with disconnected towns a handful of  marauding monsters and resource-rich  islands that randomly appear in the  nothingness your character is one of an  army of identical projections that serve  as the worker drones of the Soviet  themed society each projection is  controlled by another human player for  some reason you'll only catch brief  ethereal glimpses of other players  rather than simply playing alongside  them at all times but you'll feel their  presence as your resource counts  fluctuate and new buildings pop up in  your shared town resource gathering  constitutes the single largest portion  of the moment-to-moment gameplay but  every single step in the process is  excruciatingly ly tedious first you must  travel from your town to the one  available island at that moment which  means waiting for the bus you cannot  call the bus as it runs on a preset  schedule and there is only one bus if  you wander up as it's pulling away too  bad and once you board the bus you  cannot skip the ride if you hate  unskipable cutscenesthat's  once you actually reach the island you  can start to break it's odd destructible  environments down into resources  unfortunately with the basic pickaxe you  hit a button once then wait is your  projection hammers away and a meter  slowly fills it's painful you can use  real cash to purchase a black-market  pickaxe that is according to the menu  text 15 point 5 times faster than the  basic pickaxe but be warned all tools  even those for purchase tools break over  time once you uncover some raw materials  you have to drag each block back to the  bus stop using a backpack that contains  just three pockets for resources which  obviously results in endless unavoidable  backtracking that is utterly mindless  and repetitive then you have to wait for  the bus to return before manually  hauling all the resources from the  original bus stop to their individual  piles further into town yet another  tiresome empty process that could have  been easily automated the islands  themselves can be visually intriguing  and even fun to explore but that's  nowhere near enough to compensate for  the mind-numbing time devouring resource  collection loop of course all that  resource gathering is in service of the  games other major gameplay pillar  crafting if the city building portion of  tomorrow children was brilliant and  rewarding maybe just maybe all that  tedious mining and moving and piling  would feel worthwhile unfortunately it's  not at all basically every town you  could possibly find yourself in will  already have most of its essential  infrastructure so at best you'll need to  add a power plant or maybe a new  apartment complex or a turret the  crafting table simply doesn't offer many  options that are actually worth building  which stymies any strategy that might  have emerged from this portion of the  game plus you're always at the mercy of  the islands which may not provide the  resources you need outside of crafting  towns also team with civics related  stats &amp; gameplay systems but none of  these ancillary systems seem to have any  material impact on the experience  elections leveling perks my towns  technology rating their menus explained  the numerical implications of each one  but I never actually felt that impact  while playing the game  I also never felt attached to my town in  any way  remember in tomorrow children you're  building a town with online strangers  with whom you cannot communicate as a  result towns develop haphazard layouts  that are difficult to navigate much less  take pride in if nothing else tomorrow  children's style is certainly novel from  its creepy doll face characters to its  patriotic propaganda the game certainly  makes the most of its chosen motif  unfortunately that motif is much cooler  on paper than in practice after hearing  the same Soviet March blare at me from a  teleprompter for the hundredth time I  was more than ready to leave that world  forever I simply did not want to spend  time there it also didn't help that the  game suffers from persistent noticeable  technical problems but frankly poor  collision detection is the least of  tomorrow children's troubles the  individual components aren't enjoyable  on their own and they don't contribute  to anything bigger  there is no payoff there is no point and  I'm happy to be done playing it  [Music]  by travelpod member  [Music]

